# the argument of hobbes-leviathan-2.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Hobbes, on who interprets written laws. "May erre" and "can change" are read as plain claims, not modal ones.
atom	cavil	commentaries are commonly more subject to cavill than the Text
atom	need_more	commentaries need other commentaries
atom	endless	there will be no end of such Interpretation
atom	comm_interp	he that writeth a Commentary upon written Lawes is the Interpreter of them
atom	auth	there is an Interpreter authorised by the Soveraign, from which the subordinate Judges are not to recede
atom	judges_interp	the Interpreter of written Lawes is the ordinary Judges
atom	law_case	the Judges' Sentences are to be taken by them that plead for Lawes in that particular case
atom	bind_others	the Judges' Sentences bind other Judges in like cases to give like judgements
atom	may_err	a Judge may erre in the Interpretation even of written Lawes
atom	err_change	an errour of a subordinate Judge can change the Law
P1	S2	cavil	kind empirical
P2	S2	cavil → need_more
P3	S2	need_more → endless
A1	-	endless → ¬comm_interp
C1	S1	¬comm_interp	from P1 P2 P3 A1
A2	-	¬auth ∧ ¬comm_interp → judges_interp
C2	S3	¬auth → judges_interp	from C1 A2
P4	S3	law_case	kind normative
P5	S4	may_err
P6	S4	¬err_change
A3	-	may_err ∧ ¬err_change → ¬bind_others
C	S3	(¬auth → judges_interp) ∧ law_case ∧ ¬bind_others	from C2 P4 P5 P6 A3
